// `Result` and the interface it implements.
//
// tests/samples/stdlib/typing.fin is the specification. This copy keeps the enum, the
// interface and the implements block; what it drops is the `ErrorLike` union bound on
// `Result`'s second generic parameter, because the draft spells it
// `string | any implements <Error>` and this file would then have to import `Error`
// from a module that imports nothing back -- a cycle the loader has no rule for yet.
// The bound is a hint, not a constraint the corpus depends on.
//
// The draft's `Result<T, U> implements <IResult> { ... }` block is dropped for a
// second reason, and one reason only: `keyidof(Ok)`, which every one of its three
// bodies guards on, reports `expected '$enum_member', got 'fn(T) -> Result<T, U>'` --
// a payloaded member named rather than called is its constructor, and whether that
// satisfies the `$enum_member` meta-type is an owner ruling stdlib/typing.fin's note
// records as undecided.
//
// The two reasons that used to stand here are gone. An enum *can* be the target of an
// implements block, and the block declares its target's generic parameters, so every
// `T` and `U` in the bodies' own signatures resolves; and the bodies' `enum_.0` reads
// too -- positional payload access is slot N of whichever member the value holds
// (Soundness_PositionalMembers), and raising one is the raise form of `blame` rather
// than the assert form (Soundness_Blame.RaisingAValueOfErasedTypeIsAccepted). What is
// left is one expression, so `IResult` is declared and unimplemented here, exactly as
// far as the compiler goes.
#[stdimport] %{
import { getkeyid, keyidof } from enums::std;
}%

namespace std {

#[llvm_name="Result"]
pub enum Result <T, U> {
    Ok(T),
    Err(U),
}

#[export]
pub interface IResult<T, U> {
    pub:
      fun unwrap(enum_ : Result<T, U>) <T>;
      fun expect(enum_ : Result<T, U>) <noret>;
      fun select(enum_ : Result<T, U>, callback : fn(T) -> void) <noret>;
}

}
